{#- Model card: the own mix model backend. -#}
{% import '_macros.j2' as m %}
{% set card = 'mmm-own' %}
{% set title = "own, the repository's marketing mix model" %}
{% set floor_all = raw('recovery.own.best_condition_error') > raw('recovery.own.error_floor') %}
{% set conditions = ['rho_02_feedback_off', 'rho_02_feedback_on', 'rho_06_feedback_off', 'rho_06_feedback_on', 'rho_09_feedback_off', 'rho_09_feedback_on'] %}
{% set rank = namespace(lowest_two_at_high_rho=true) %}
{% for c in conditions if not c.startswith('rho_09') %}
{% if raw('recovery.own.' ~ c ~ '.rank_agreement') <= [raw('recovery.own.rho_09_feedback_on.rank_agreement'), raw('recovery.own.rho_09_feedback_off.rank_agreement')]|max %}{% set rank.lowest_two_at_high_rho = false %}{% endif %}
{% endfor %}
{% set intended %}
The own backend estimates what each of Alderquist's {{ v('policy.channel_count') }} paid channels
returns per dollar, how long its effect lasts and where it saturates, from a weekly panel of spend
and sales, so that the budget optimizer can move money until the next dollar earns the same in
every channel; it is the backend the quarterly review's plan is built on and the one the API
serves. It is meant for reallocating a stated budget across these channels within the stated
constraints. It is not meant for forecasting sales, for judging a single campaign or creative, for
a channel it was not fit on, for a budget far outside the spend it has seen (its curves are
extrapolations there), or for any real brand before it has been fit and graded on that brand's
own panel.
{% endset %}
{% set data %}
The demonstration brand's national weekly panel: {{ v('sim.weeks') }} weeks from
{{ v('sim.first_week') }} to {{ v('sim.last_week') }}, summed over {{ v('sim.geos') }} simulated geos, with
spend in each channel, sales, a price index, promotions and holidays. Spend averages
{{ v('sim.spend_weekly_mean') }} a week and sales {{ v('sim.sales_weekly_mean') }}. The market is
simulated under the demonstration condition, {{ v('sim.condition') }}: the channels' weekly spend is
correlated (a target of {{ v('sim.spend_correlation_target') }}, {{ v('sim.spend_correlation_achieved') }}
achieved) and retargeting spend follows last week's demand. The truth is known:
{{ v('sim.incremental_share') }} of sales are caused by paid media and {{ v('sim.baseline_share') }} are
baseline. The true channel parameters the model is trying to recover:

{{ table('sim.truth_channels') }}

{{ label('sim.truth_channels') }}
{% endset %}
{% set fit %}
Sales in a week are organic demand, multiplicative in trend, price, promotion, holidays and
seasonality, plus each channel's adstocked and saturated spend times a non negative ceiling
(`marginal_mmm.own`). Carryover is geometric; saturation is a Hill curve with a half saturation
point and a slope. The transforms are searched in stated steps: a grid over carryover, half
saturation and slope, one channel at a time ({{ v('mmm.own.grid_evaluations') }} evaluations); a ridge
penalty chosen on forecast error at {{ v('policy.rolling_origins') }} rolling origins held out in time;
then Nelder-Mead from the grid optimum, channel by channel and then jointly
({{ v('mmm.own.nelder_mead_evaluations') }} evaluations). The Nelder-Mead step ends at its evaluation cap
and does not report convergence, on this fit and on every recovery fit
(`results/mmm/own_diagnostics.json`, `results/recovery/own_diagnostics.json`). Intervals come from a
moving block bootstrap over weeks, {{ v('mmm.own.bootstrap_replicates') }} replicates of
{{ v('policy.bootstrap_block_weeks') }} week blocks at the {{ v('policy.interval_level') }} level, with the
transforms held at their searched values. The fit took {{ v('mmm.own.fit_seconds') }} seconds; its spec
hash is `{{ v('mmm.own.spec_hash') }}`.

Calibration adds the posted lift result as a pseudo observation: the lift the model implies for the
test's geos and weeks is pulled toward the experiment's estimate, with a weight set by the
experiment's precision against a stated residual scale, and the search runs again with that row in
place. The calibrated export, `{{ v('budget.model_version') }}`, is the one the plan and the API use.
{% endset %}
{% set metrics %}
Return on ad spend on the demonstration brand, with the marginal return at current spend and the
half life of carryover, each beside the truth:

{{ table('mmm.own.returns') }}

{{ label('mmm.own.returns') }}

The intervals cover the truth in {{ v('mmm.own.covered_count') }} of {{ v('policy.channel_count') }}
channels. The model puts {{ v('mmm.own.baseline_share') }} of sales in the baseline against a true
{{ v('sim.baseline_share') }}, leaves {{ v('mmm.own.residual_share') }} of sales unexplained, and has an
unexplained variance share of {{ v('mmm.own.unexplained_variance') }}.

**Recovery.** Refit on markets with known truth, {{ v('recovery.own.seeds_per_condition') }} seeds in
each of {{ v('recovery.own.conditions') }} conditions ({{ v('recovery.own.fits_that_ran') }} of
{{ v('recovery.own.fits') }} fits ran), the median absolute error in return across channels is
{{ v('recovery.own.median_abs_error_overall') }} overall, from {{ v('recovery.own.best_condition_error') }}
({{ v('recovery.own.best_condition') }}) to {{ v('recovery.own.worst_condition_error') }} in the worst
condition ({{ v('recovery.own.worst_condition') }}){% if floor_all %}, over the stated
{{ v('recovery.own.error_floor') }} floor in every condition{% endif %}. Intervals hold the truth
{{ v('recovery.own.coverage_overall') }} of the time against a stated {{ v('policy.interval_level') }}.
{% if rank.lowest_two_at_high_rho %}
Rank agreement with the true order of the channels is lowest where the channels' spend moves most
